FUSION_ANSWER NATIVE8192_TOP2_H1 H1 'The provided text discusses several studies that explore the impact of generative AI (GenAI) on learning and creative thinking. Here are the main conclusions of the three key publications and where they disagree:\n\n1. **Mariñoso et al. (2025)**:\n   - **Conclusion**: Found that an AI-assisted group of undergraduate early-childhood-education students achieved significantly higher academic scores in designing mathematical learning situations compared to a control group using traditional resources.\n   - **Key Point**: AI can reduce extraneous processing demands, freeing cognitive resources for higher-level conceptual work.\n\n2. **Peng et al. (2025)**:\n   - **Conclusion**: Reported a moderate positive short-term effect of AI-supported learning on creative thinking (SMD = 0.54) based on a meta-analysis of 56 experimental studies.\n   - **Key Point**: AI can enhance creative thinking, but there is substantial heterogeneity in effect sizes and a lack of long-term follow-up assessments.\n\n3. **Wang and Wu (2025)**:\n   - **Conclusion**: Identified three qualitative themes in a mixed-methods study with 363 undergraduate design students: (a) students valued AI’s accessibility and efficiency; (b) students identified gaps in depth, interpretation, and critical thinking; (c) students engaged in critical trust and verification behaviors.\n   - **Key Point**: AI may promote surface-level processing and reduce deep cognitive engagement, as students rely on AI to bypass cognitive struggle.\n\n### Disagreements:\n- **Efficiency vs. Depth**:\n  - **Mariñoso et al.** and **Peng et al.** emphasize the positive effects of AI on performance and creative thinking, respectively.\n  - **Wang and Wu** highlight the potential for AI to promote surface-level processing and reduce deep cognitive engagement, suggesting a trade-off between efficiency'
