FUSION_ANSWER LEGACY_MEAN_H2 H2 "The claims supported by empirical evidence include:\n\n1. **Performance Gains**: Studies like those by Mariñoso et al. (2025) and Peng et al. (2025) show that AI-assisted students can achieve higher academic scores and demonstrate a moderate positive short-term effect on creative thinking. These findings suggest that GenAI can enhance performance in specific tasks.\n\n2. **Positive Attitudes**: Research by Pan and Li (2025) indicates that positive attitudes toward GenAI mediate the relationship between teacher support and learner well-being, suggesting that AI tools can enhance intrinsic motivation and well-being.\n\n3. **Critical Thinking Paradox**: Process-sensitive research by Wang and Wu (2025) and Choudhuri et al. (2026) reveals that while students value AI's efficiency, they also identify gaps in depth, interpretation, and critical thinking. This suggests that AI may promote surface-level processing and reduce the cognitive struggle necessary for deep conceptual understanding.\n\nThe main limitations of these findings include:\n\n1. **Methodological Focus on Product Outcomes**: Most studies primarily measure product quality (e.g., assignment grades, self-reported attitudes) rather than the processes that lead to durable, transferable knowledge. This limits the ability to determine whether AI enhances long-term learning.\n\n2. **Heterogeneity in Findings**: There is substantial variation in effect sizes and a lack of long-term follow-up assessments, making it difficult to generalize the findings.\n\n3. **Cognitive Load and Individual Differences**: The rapid adoption of GenAI has proceeded without sufficient attention to managing cognitive load and accounting for individual differences between novice and expert learners. This can lead to suboptimal learning outcomes.\n\n4. **Shallow Processing Concerns**: Process-sensitive research indicates that AI may promote surface-level processing and reduce the cognitive struggle necessary for deep understanding. Students may engage in critical trust and"
