FUSION_ANSWER NATIVE8192_MAXMEAN_H2 H2 "The claims supported by empirical evidence include:\n\n1. **Performance Gains**: Studies show that AI-assisted students can achieve higher academic scores compared to those using traditional resources (Mariñoso et al., 2025). This is consistent with the cognitive load theory, which suggests that AI can reduce extraneous processing demands, allowing learners to allocate more cognitive resources to higher-level conceptual work (Sweller et al., 2011).\n\n2. **Positive Attitudes and Well-Being**: Positive attitudes toward GenAI can mediate the relationship between teacher support and learner well-being (Pan and Li, 2025). This aligns with the idea that AI tools supporting learners' needs for autonomy, competence, and relatedness can enhance intrinsic motivation (Ryan and Deci, 2000).\n\n3. **Moderate Short-Term Effect on Creative Thinking**: A meta-analysis of 56 experimental studies reported a moderate positive short-term effect of AI-supported learning on creative thinking (Peng et al., 2025). However, the effect sizes were heterogeneous, and there was a lack of long-term follow-up assessments.\n\n4. **Limitations in Critical Thinking Depth**: Process-oriented mixed-methods research has identified gaps in depth, interpretation, and critical thinking when students use AI (Wang and Wu, 2025). Students engage in compensatory verification behaviors, cross-checking AI outputs against external sources due to perceived interpretive shallowness.\n\n5. **Negative Association with Reflection and Critical Thinking**: Trust-driven routine GenAI use was negatively associated with reflection, critical thinking, and the need for understanding (Choudhuri et al., 2026). This is particularly evident among students with higher technophilic motivations and greater computer self-efficacy.\n\n### Main Limitations:\n\n1. **Outcome Measures**: Many studies primarily measure product quality"
